<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georgius Agricola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1494">1494</a>–<a href="/years/1555">1555</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Georg Agricola, Georg Bauer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georgius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a> was a German physician and scholar, often called the father of mineralogy. Working as a town doctor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jachymov">Joachimsthal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemnitz">Chemnitz</a>, he studied the mines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Ore Mountains</a> and wrote De re metallica, published in 1556, a year after his death: a systematic account of mining and metallurgy, illustrated with woodcuts, that remained the standard work for two centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georgius-agricola.jpg" alt="Georgius Agricola" width="310" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georgius_Agricola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mining brought technical expertise, recorded in <b>Georg Agricola</b>’s De re metallica of 1556, and the Freiberg Mining Academy, founded in 1765, became a leading school of mining science.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glauchau">Glauchau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemnitz">Chemnitz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.82,12.54&amp;year=1533" aria-label="Glauchau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4835 400 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Georgius Agricola"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5285 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5285 670h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.82,12.54&amp;year=1533">What was here in 1533? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Since the art of mining does not lend itself to elegant language, these books of mine are correspondingly lacking in refinement of style.&#8221;</p><footer>De Re Metallica (1556), preface &#183; tr. Herbert Clark Hoover and Lou Henry Hoover, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/38015/pg38015-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And when the woods and groves are felled, then are exterminated the beasts and birds, very many of which furnish a pleasant and agreeable food for man.&#8221;</p><footer>De Re Metallica (1556), book 1 &#183; tr. Herbert Clark Hoover and Lou Henry Hoover, 1912 &#183; the critics' case against mining, which he sets out before answering it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/38015/pg38015-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Further, when the ores are washed, the water which has been used poisons the brooks and streams, and either destroys the fish or drives them away.&#8221;</p><footer>De Re Metallica (1556), book 1 &#183; tr. Herbert Clark Hoover and Lou Henry Hoover, 1912 &#183; the critics' case against mining, which he sets out before answering it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/38015/pg38015-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
